<h2 id="4-managing-events">4. Managing Events</h2>
<p>Events are things like Sunday school, fellowship dinners, community
service days, and committee meetings. You can add new events, edit
existing ones, and remove events that have passed or been cancelled.</p>
<h3 id="viewing-events">Viewing Events</h3>
<p>Click <strong>Events</strong> in the navigation bar. You will see a
list of upcoming events. At the top of the list, there are filter
buttons: <strong>All</strong>, <strong>Education</strong>,
<strong>Fellowship</strong>, <strong>Meetings</strong>, and
<strong>Community Service</strong>. Clicking one of those buttons shows
only events in that category.</p>
<h3 id="adding-a-new-event">Adding a New Event</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>Click <strong>Events</strong> in the navigation bar.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>New Event</strong> button in the top right corner
of the page.</li>
<li>Fill in the form (details below).</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Create Event</strong> button at the bottom.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>What each field means:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>What to enter</th>
<th>Required?</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Title</strong></td>
<td>The name of the event, e.g. "Men's Breakfast"</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Description</strong></td>
<td>A sentence or two about the event. This shows up on the event's
detail page.</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Date</strong></td>
<td>The date the event takes place. Click the field and a date picker
will appear — just click the right date.</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Start Time</strong></td>
<td>When the event begins. Click the field and use the up/down arrows to
set the time.</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>End Time</strong></td>
<td>When the event ends. You can leave this blank if you don't know or
it doesn't apply.</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Location</strong></td>
<td>Where the event is held, e.g. "Fellowship Hall" or "Sanctuary".</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Category</strong></td>
<td>Choose the type of event from the dropdown list.</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Event Image</strong></td>
<td>A photo to show with the event. Click one of the thumbnail images to
choose it. Click "No image" if you don't want one.</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>About categories:</strong> Every event must belong to one of
four categories — <strong>Education</strong>,
<strong>Fellowship</strong>, <strong>Meetings</strong>, or
<strong>Community Service</strong>. This helps visitors find events that
interest them. Choose whichever fits best.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3 id="editing-an-event">Editing an Event</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>Click <strong>Events</strong> in the navigation bar.</li>
<li>Find the event you want to change and click on it to open it.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Edit</strong> button near the top right of the
event's page.</li>
<li>Make your changes.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Update Event</strong> button at the bottom.</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="deleting-an-event">Deleting an Event</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>Open the event you want to remove (same steps as editing).</li>
<li>Click the red <strong>Delete</strong> button near the top
right.</li>
<li>A small box will pop up asking "Are you sure?" — click
<strong>OK</strong> to confirm.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> Deleting an event is permanent. Once it
is gone, it is gone. Only delete events you are sure you no longer
need.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2 id="5-managing-photo-galleries">5. Managing Photo Galleries</h2>
<p>A gallery is a collection of photos grouped together — like a photo
album. You might have one for "Easter Sunday 2025" or "Fall Festival."
Galleries can be kept private while you are putting them together, then
made public when they are ready.</p>
<h3 id="viewing-galleries">Viewing Galleries</h3>
<p>Click <strong>Galleries</strong> in the navigation bar. You will see
all of your photo albums. Albums that are not yet published (not visible
to the public) are shown only when you are signed in.</p>
<h3 id="creating-a-new-gallery">Creating a New Gallery</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>Click <strong>Galleries</strong> in the navigation bar.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>New Gallery</strong> button.</li>
<li>Fill in the <strong>Title</strong> (required) — for example,
"Christmas Dinner 2024."</li>
<li>Fill in the <strong>Description</strong> if you like — a short
sentence about what the photos are from.</li>
<li>The <strong>Published</strong> checkbox controls whether the public
can see this gallery. Leave it unchecked for now if you are still adding
photos to it.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Create Gallery</strong> button.</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="adding-photos-to-a-gallery">Adding Photos to a Gallery</h3>
<p>Before you can add photos to a gallery, the photos need to be in your
<strong>Image Library</strong> first. Think of the Image Library like a
filing cabinet — you have to put photos in the filing cabinet before you
can put them in an album.</p>
<p>If you have not uploaded your photos yet, see <strong>Section 7: The
Image Library</strong> first, then come back here.</p>
<p>Once your photos are in the Image Library:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li>Open the gallery you want to add photos to (click on it from the
Galleries page).</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Edit</strong> button.</li>
<li>Scroll down past the gallery details form. You will see a grid of
your available photos.</li>
<li>Click each photo you want to include in this gallery. A selected
photo will get an amber-colored border around it.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Update Gallery</strong> button to save.</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="changing-the-order-of-photos">Changing the Order of Photos</h3>
<p>When you are editing a gallery, the photos currently in it are shown
in a list at the top of the edit page. You can drag them into a
different order — just click and hold a photo, drag it to where you want
it, and let go. The order saves automatically.</p>
<h3 id="publishing-a-gallery-making-it-visible-to-the-public">Publishing
a Gallery (Making It Visible to the Public)</h3>
<p>A gallery is invisible to the public until you publish it. This is
useful when you are still adding or arranging photos and don't want
anyone to see an unfinished album yet.</p>
<p>To publish a gallery:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li>Open the gallery.</li>
<li>Click <strong>Edit</strong>.</li>
<li>Check the box next to <strong>Published (visible to the
public)</strong>.</li>
<li>Click <strong>Update Gallery</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>To hide a gallery again, follow the same steps and uncheck the
box.</p>
<h3 id="deleting-a-gallery">Deleting a Gallery</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>Open the gallery.</li>
<li>Click the red <strong>Delete</strong> button.</li>
<li>Confirm when prompted.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> Deleting a gallery does not delete the photos
themselves. The photos stay in your Image Library and can be used in
other galleries.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2 id="6-managing-church-members">6. Managing Church Members</h2>
<p>The Members section is a private directory of church members. It is
never visible to the public — only people who are signed in as admins
can see it. Think of it as a digital version of the church
directory.</p>
<h3 id="viewing-members">Viewing Members</h3>
<p>Click <strong>Dashboard</strong> in the navigation bar, then click <strong>All Members</strong>. You will see a
list of all members sorted alphabetically by last name.</p>
<h3 id="adding-a-new-member">Adding a New Member</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>Click <strong>Dashboard</strong> in the navigation bar.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>New Member</strong> button.</li>
<li>Fill in the form (details below).</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Create Member</strong> button.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>What each field means:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Notes</th>
<th>Required?</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>First Name</strong></td>
<td>Member's first name</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Last Name</strong></td>
<td>Member's last name</td>
<td>Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Email</strong></td>
<td>Member's email address</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Phone</strong></td>
<td>Phone number — any format is fine (e.g. 225-555-1234)</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Date of Birth</strong></td>
<td>Used to display birthdays on the home page (name only — no
other member details are ever shown publicly)</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Address</strong></td>
<td>Street address, city, state, and zip code</td>
<td>No</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>About birthdays:</strong> If a member has a birthday this
month, their name and the date will automatically appear on the
home page in the "Birthdays This Month" section. This is a nice touch
for the congregation! Their other details are never shown
publicly.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3 id="editing-a-members-information">Editing a Member's
Information</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>Click <strong>Dashboard</strong> in the navigation bar, then click <strong>All Members</strong>.</li>
<li>Find the member and click their name to open their record.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Edit</strong> button.</li>
<li>Make your changes.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Update Member</strong> button.</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="removing-a-member">Removing a Member</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>Open the member's record.</li>
<li>Click the red <strong>Delete</strong> button.</li>
<li>Confirm when prompted.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2 id="7-the-image-library">7. The Image Library</h2>
<p>The Image Library is where all of your photos live on the website.
Think of it as a shared filing cabinet in the cloud — all your uploaded
photos are stored here, and you can use them in events or photo
galleries. The photos are not stored on your computer; they live on the
website and are available from anywhere.</p>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> Photos in the Image Library are not shown
to the public on their own. They only appear publicly when they are
attached to an event or included in a published gallery.</p>
<h3 id="viewing-the-image-library">Viewing the Image Library</h3>
<p>Click <strong>Dashboard</strong> in the navigation bar, then click <strong>Upload Photos</strong>. On the upload page, click the <strong>Cancel</strong> link — that takes you to the Image Library grid, where you can see all your uploaded photos. (After you finish an upload, you are also taken there automatically.)</p>
<h3 id="uploading-new-photos">Uploading New Photos</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>Click <strong>Dashboard</strong> in the navigation bar.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Upload Photos</strong> button.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Choose File</strong> button (or it might say
<strong>Browse</strong>). This opens a window on your computer where you
can find the photo files you want to upload.</li>
<li>Find the photos on your computer and click to select them. You can
select up to 20 photos at once by holding the <strong>Shift</strong> key
and clicking a range, or by holding <strong>Ctrl</strong> (on Windows)
or <strong>Command</strong> (on a Mac) and clicking individual
photos.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Upload</strong> button.</li>
</ol>
<p>The photos will be sent to the website. This may take a moment
depending on how many photos you are uploading and how fast your
internet connection is. Please be patient and do not close the browser
tab while it is uploading.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Accepted file types:</strong> JPG, JPEG, PNG, WebP, and GIF.
Most photos taken on a phone or digital camera are JPG files and will
work just fine.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip:</strong> You do not need to resize or compress your
photos before uploading. The website handles that automatically.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3 id="deleting-a-photo-from-the-library">Deleting a Photo from the
Library</h3>
<ol type="1">
<li>In the Image Library, find the photo you want to remove.</li>
<li>Click the <strong>Delete</strong> button below it.</li>
<li>Confirm when prompted.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Be careful:</strong> If a photo is being used in an event or
a gallery, deleting it will remove it from those places too. The event
or gallery will still exist, but the photo will no longer appear
there.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2 id="8-signing-out">8. Signing Out</h2>
<p>When you are done making changes, it is a good idea to log out —
especially if you are on a shared computer.</p>
<p>Look for your <strong>Log Out</strong> link in the dark footer section at
the bottom of any page. Click it and you will be logged out
immediately.</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="9-a-few-helpful-reminders">9. A Few Helpful Reminders</h2>
<p><strong>Nothing is instant.</strong> After you save a change, you may
need to refresh the page to see the updated version. You can refresh by
pressing <strong>F5</strong> on Windows or <strong>Command + R</strong>
on a Mac, or by clicking the circular arrow icon in your browser.</p>
<p><strong>Red boxes mean something needs to be fixed.</strong> If you
try to save a form and something was filled out wrong (or a required
field was left blank), a red box will appear at the top of the form
listing what needs to be corrected. Just fix those items and try saving
again.</p>
<p><strong>You won't accidentally publish something by saving a
draft.</strong> Galleries are only visible to the public after you check
the "Published" box and save. Events are visible immediately when you
save them — so only create an event when you are ready for the public to
see it.</p>
<p><strong>When in doubt, ask for help.</strong> If something doesn't
look right or you aren't sure what to do, don't keep clicking around
hoping something changes. Close the tab, take a breath, and reach out to
whoever set up the website for you.</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="10-social-media-sharing">10. Social Media Sharing</h2>
<p>When someone copies a link to the church website and shares it on
social media — such as Facebook, X (formerly Twitter), or in a text
message — the platform automatically shows a preview card. This card
typically includes a photo, a title, and a short description of the
page.</p>
<p>The website handles this automatically. You do not need to do anything
to make it work. Every page on the site is already set up to provide the
right information to social media platforms when a link is shared.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> The photo that appears in the social media
preview is a church image chosen for the site. The title and description
are generated from the page you are sharing. If you ever feel the
preview looks wrong or you would like to update the default photo, reach
out to whoever manages the technical side of the website.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2 id="11-contact-form-messages">11. Contact Form Messages</h2>
<p>The <strong>Contact Us</strong> page includes a form that anyone
visiting the website can use to send a message to the church. When
someone fills it out and clicks <strong>Send Message</strong>, the
website automatically sends an email to the church's designated contact
address.</p>
<p><strong>You do not need to log in or check anything inside the admin
area.</strong> The message arrives directly in your email inbox, just
like any other email.</p>
<p>The email will show:</p>
<ul>
<li>The visitor's <strong>name</strong></li>
<li>The visitor's <strong>email address</strong></li>
<li>Their <strong>message</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>To reply, simply hit <strong>Reply</strong> in your email client. The
reply will go directly to the visitor — you do not need to copy or paste
their address.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> If you ever need to change the email address
that contact form messages are sent to, ask whoever manages the technical
side of the website. It is a one-line change in the code.</p>
</blockquote>
